import lib/process.dl

to get the binary process test interpreter:
    return "python" if the process platform is Windows, else "python3"

set input to a new string buffer containing 3 bytes
store 65 as a byte into the data of input at 0
store 0 as a byte into the data of input at 1
store 66 as a byte into the data of input at 2

set command to a process command for the binary process test interpreter
add "-c" as an argument to command
add "import sys; data=sys.stdin.buffer.read(); sys.stdout.buffer.write(data); sys.stderr.buffer.write(bytes([69,0,70]))" as an argument to command
run command using input and set result to it

print lines (result was launched), (result succeeded) and (result's exit code = (0 as a 64 bit integer))
print lines (result's standard output's length = 3) and (the character at 0 in result's standard output = 65)
print line (the character at 1 in result's standard output = 0)
print lines (the character at 2 in result's standard output = 66) and (result's standard error's length = 3)
print lines (the character at 0 in result's standard error = 69) and (the character at 1 in result's standard error = 0)
print line (the character at 2 in result's standard error = 70)
